### Why is serialization of sealed types straightforward?

Serialization (object to JSON) works because the library just reads the concrete object's fields. The sealed hierarchy does not add complexity on the write side.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@JsonTypeInfo(use = JsonTypeInfo.Id.NAME, property = "type")
@JsonSubTypes({
    @JsonSubTypes.Type(value = SavingsAccount.class, name = "SAVINGS"),
    @JsonSubTypes.Type(value = CurrentAccount.class, name = "CURRENT"),
    @JsonSubTypes.Type(value = LoanAccount.class, name = "LOAN")
})
public sealed interface Account
        permits SavingsAccount, CurrentAccount, LoanAccount {
    String id();
    BigDecimal balance();
}
```

### How does Jackson polymorphic deserialization work?

Jackson uses `@JsonTypeInfo` and `@JsonSubTypes` to map a discriminator value to the correct concrete class. The discriminator is written as a property in the JSON.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public record SavingsAccount(String id, BigDecimal balance)
        implements Account {}

public record CurrentAccount(String id, BigDecimal balance)
        implements Account {}

public record LoanAccount(String id, BigDecimal balance)
        implements Account {}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.*;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class JacksonSealedDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();

        Account savings = new SavingsAccount("SAV-1001", new BigDecimal("500000"));

        // Serialize: writes the type discriminator
        String json = mapper.writeValueAsString(savings);
        System.out.println("JSON: " + json);

        // Deserialize: reads the discriminator to pick the subtype
        Account restored = mapper.readValue(json, Account.class);
        System.out.println("Type: " + restored.getClass().getSimpleName());
        System.out.println("Balance: " + restored.balance());
    }
}
```

```text
JSON: {"type":"SAVINGS","id":"SAV-1001","balance":500000}
Type: SavingsAccount
Balance: 500000
```

### How does Gson handle sealed hierarchies?

Gson has no built-in polymorphic deserialization. You register a `RuntimeTypeAdapterFactory` from the extras companion.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.*;
import com.google.gson.Gson;
import com.google.gson.GsonBuilder;
import com.google.gson.typeadapters.RuntimeTypeAdapterFactory;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class GsonSealedDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RuntimeTypeAdapterFactory<Account> factory =
                RuntimeTypeAdapterFactory.of(Account.class, "type")
                        .registerSubtype(SavingsAccount.class, "SAVINGS")
                        .registerSubtype(CurrentAccount.class, "CURRENT")
                        .registerSubtype(LoanAccount.class, "LOAN");

        Gson gson = new GsonBuilder()
                .registerTypeAdapterFactory(factory)
                .create();

        String json = "{\"type\":\"SAVINGS\",\"id\":\"SAV-1001\",\"balance\":500000}";
        Account restored = gson.fromJson(json, Account.class);
        System.out.println("Type: " + restored.getClass().getSimpleName());
    }
}
```

### What are the key caveats?

Four main caveats apply to serialization of sealed hierarchies.

| Caveat | Description |
|---|---|
| Discriminator required | Must embed a type property and agree on values across all producers and consumers |
| Subtype sync | Registered/annotated subtypes must stay in sync with `permits` |
| Record binding | Records have no setters; library must use canonical constructors |
| Security | Never enable Jackson default typing on untrusted JSON |

```java
// DANGEROUS: default typing accepts arbitrary class names
// mapper.enableDefaultTyping(); // NEVER do this with untrusted JSON!

// SAFE: controlled Id.NAME mapping
// @JsonTypeInfo(use = JsonTypeInfo.Id.NAME, property = "type")
// Only your known subtypes are accepted
```

### How do records work as sealed leaves with Jackson?

Jackson binds records through their canonical constructor. Each component becomes a property. Since records have no setters, Jackson must use constructor-based binding, which it supports natively since Jackson 2.12.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// Record: immutable, no setters, constructor-based binding
public record SavingsAccount(String id, BigDecimal balance)
        implements Account {
    // Jackson uses the canonical constructor:
    // SavingsAccount(String id, BigDecimal balance)
}
```

### What about versioning across service boundaries?

Adding a new permitted subtype is a wire-compatibility change. Old consumers that do not know the new type will fail to deserialize it. Treat this like any schema evolution: version the discriminator, use a registry, and test backward compatibility.

```java
// Version 1: Account permits SAVINGS, CURRENT, LOAN
// Version 2: Account permits SAVINGS, CURRENT, LOAN, FIXED_DEPOSIT

// Old consumer receiving {"type":"FIXED_DEPOSIT",...}:
// Jackson: InvalidTypeIdException (unknown type id)
// Gson: JsonParseException

// Solution: version the API, use a fallback handler
```

### How does sealing help serialization?

Sealing helps because the closed `permits` list is the authoritative set the serializer must support. You can derive or validate the registered subtypes against it, and modern Jackson's inference leans on this.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import java.util.Arrays;

public class PermittedSubtypeSync {
    public static void validateSerializerConfig() {
        Class<?>[] permitted = Account.class.getPermittedSubclasses();
        System.out.println("Serializer must support:");
        Arrays.stream(permitted)
                .forEach(c -> System.out.println("  " + c.getSimpleName()));
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

For internal, trusted JSON you can rely on Jackson's permits inference. For external or cross-organisation JSON, keep an explicit, reviewed discriminator mapping. In banking, Pankaj at National Bank of India never enables default typing on external APIs and always validates that the Jackson subtype annotations match the sealed `permits` list at startup.
